CURVEBALLS = {
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-acute-otitis-externa': {
        "curveball": 'What if this swimmer had a known tympanic membrane perforation, or turned out to be a poorly controlled diabetic? How would your drop choice and plan change?',
        "curveball_answer": 'With a known or possible perforation or tube, use a non-ototoxic fluoroquinolone drop such as ofloxacin or ciprofloxacin and avoid aminoglycoside, alcohol and acidifying drops. Diabetes or immunocompromise raises concern for necrotizing otitis externa and fungal infection: look for granulation at the bony-cartilaginous junction and cranial neuropathy, and follow closely. Per the 2014 AOE guideline, add systemic antibiotics only for spread beyond the canal or a high-risk host.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-age-related-hearing-loss-presbycusis': {
        "curveball": 'A presbycusis patient wearing well-fitted hearing aids now scores poorly on aided sentence testing. What is your next step, and how do you counsel about age?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Refer for cochlear implant candidacy evaluation. The 2024 age-related hearing loss guideline supports CI evaluation when appropriately fitted hearing aids no longer give adequate speech understanding. First verify the fitting with real-ear measurement. Age alone is not a contraindication: older adults gain meaningful speech and quality-of-life benefit and usually tolerate surgery well. Outcomes depend more on duration of deafness, cognition and rehabilitation than on age.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-age-related-hearing-loss-presbycusis': {
        "curveball": 'What if this patient instead had word recognition far worse than his thresholds predict? Which presbycusis type fits, and what must you exclude first?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Disproportionately poor word recognition fits neural presbycusis, from loss of spiral ganglion neurons. Before giving that label, check symmetry: asymmetric thresholds or asymmetric word recognition call for gadolinium MRI of the internal auditory canals to exclude a retrocochlear lesion such as vestibular schwannoma. Neural presbycusis predicts limited hearing-aid benefit, so counsel realistic expectations and add communication strategies and remote-microphone technology.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-audiogram-interpretation': {
        "curveball": 'What if the speech reception threshold is 20 dB better than the pure-tone average in the poorer ear? What does that suggest, and what do you do before imaging?',
        "curveball_answer": 'The speech reception threshold should agree with the pure-tone average within about 10 dB. A large mismatch, with speech better than tones, suggests nonorganic (functional) hearing loss or unreliable responses. Reinstruct and retest, run a Stenger test for a unilateral claim, and use objective measures such as acoustic reflexes, otoacoustic emissions and ABR thresholds before ordering MRI or making device decisions.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-audiogram-interpretation': {
        "curveball": 'What if the same 45 dB air-bone gap were present but the tympanogram were a normal type A with absent acoustic reflexes? What would you suspect?',
        "curveball_answer": 'An aerated middle ear with a large conductive gap points to the ossicular chain. A normal or shallow (As) tracing with absent reflexes suggests stapes fixation from otosclerosis; a very tall (Ad) tracing suggests ossicular discontinuity. If bone conduction is better than 0 dB and reflexes are present, think of a third-window lesion such as superior canal dehiscence, which stapes surgery would not fix. CT temporal bone helps sort these out.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-audiologic-electrophysiology-abr-oae-ecog': {
        "curveball": 'An adult with asymmetric sensorineural loss cannot have an MRI. What role does ABR play, and what are its limits?',
        "curveball_answer": 'ABR is the accepted fallback screen when MRI is contraindicated. A prolonged interaural wave V latency difference or a long I-V interval suggests a retrocochlear lesion. But ABR misses many small intracanalicular tumors and becomes uninterpretable with severe high-frequency loss, so a normal ABR does not exclude a small vestibular schwannoma. Gadolinium MRI remains the gold standard; consider CT with contrast or serial audiometry when MRI stays impossible.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-audiologic-electrophysiology-abr-oae-ecog': {
        "curveball": 'Why could this NICU infant pass a newborn screen done with otoacoustic emissions alone, and how should NICU screening differ from well-baby screening?',
        "curveball_answer": 'OAEs test outer hair cells, which are intact in auditory neuropathy, so an OAE-only screen passes these infants. The Joint Committee on Infant Hearing recommends automated ABR screening for infants in the NICU longer than 5 days because of their higher risk of neural disorders; well babies may be screened with OAE or automated ABR. NICU infants who fail automated ABR go straight to diagnostic ABR rather than an OAE rescreen.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuroanatomy-cochlear-physiology': {
        "curveball": 'A patient has a unilateral stroke of the auditory cortex. Why are pure-tone thresholds usually near normal, and what deficits would you expect instead?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Above the cochlear nuclei the auditory pathway projects bilaterally, crossing at the trapezoid body, lateral lemniscus and inferior colliculus. A unilateral lesion above the cochlear nucleus therefore rarely causes unilateral deafness. Expect central processing deficits: trouble with speech in noise, sound localization and dichotic listening. True unilateral deafness localizes to the cochlea, cochlear nerve or cochlear nucleus.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuroanatomy-cochlear-physiology': {
        "curveball": 'Which common drug produces reversible outer hair cell dysfunction like this, and how does its hearing loss differ from aminoglycoside or cisplatin toxicity?',
        "curveball_answer": 'High-dose salicylates reversibly impair prestin-driven outer hair cell electromotility, causing a mild-to-moderate flat loss and tinnitus that resolve within days of stopping the drug. Aminoglycosides and cisplatin kill hair cells, starting at the basal turn, so the loss is permanent and begins in the high frequencies; gentamicin is also strongly vestibulotoxic. Loop diuretics act on the stria vascularis and are usually reversible.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuropathy-spectrum-disorder': {
        "curveball": 'The parents of an infant with ANSD ask about going straight to a cochlear implant. What imaging comes first, and what finding would change candidacy?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Obtain MRI of the internal auditory canals to look for cochlear nerve deficiency, which is common in ANSD. An absent or severely hypoplastic nerve predicts poor implant benefit and may lead to auditory brainstem implant discussion. If the nerve is present, a structured hearing-aid trial with functional monitoring comes first; poor auditory and language progress despite the trial supports implantation. Some infants, for example after hyperbilirubinemia, improve, so avoid premature decisions.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuropathy-spectrum-disorder': {
        "curveball": 'Why would a child whose auditory neuropathy comes from cochlear nerve deficiency do much worse with an implant than this OTOF child?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Otoferlin is needed for synaptic vesicle release at the inner hair cell ribbon synapse. In OTOF disease the spiral ganglion and nerve are intact, so electrical stimulation bypasses the presynaptic defect and results are generally good. In cochlear nerve deficiency the defect lies in the very neural elements the electrode must stimulate, so implant outcomes are poor and an auditory brainstem implant may be considered. Site of lesion predicts implant benefit.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-autoimmune-inner-ear-disease': {
        "curveball": 'A patient with suspected autoimmune inner ear disease responds to prednisone but relapses with every taper. What is your next step?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Steroid dependence is common. Options include intratympanic steroid for local control and steroid-sparing therapy coordinated with rheumatology, though evidence for agents such as methotrexate is weak. Screen for systemic autoimmune disease such as Cogan syndrome, granulomatosis with polyangiitis or lupus. Follow serial audiograms, fit hearing aids, and refer early for cochlear implant evaluation if hearing declines despite treatment.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-autoimmune-inner-ear-disease': {
        "curveball": 'A young adult has rapidly progressive bilateral sensorineural loss plus red, painful eyes and vertigo attacks. What syndrome must you consider, and why is it urgent?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Cogan syndrome: interstitial keratitis with audiovestibular attacks that resemble Meniere disease and can progress quickly to bilateral deafness. Refer urgently to ophthalmology and rheumatology, start high-dose systemic corticosteroids promptly, and screen for systemic vasculitis, especially aortitis. Many patients still progress to profound loss and do well with cochlear implants, so start that evaluation early.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-bppv': {
        "curveball": 'The Dix-Hallpike is negative, but the supine roll test produces horizontal geotropic nystagmus. Which canal, which side, and which maneuver?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Horizontal geotropic nystagmus means lateral canal canalithiasis, and the affected ear is the side with the stronger nystagmus. Treat with a barbecue (Lempert) roll toward the healthy side or a Gufoni maneuver. Apogeotropic nystagmus suggests cupulolithiasis, where the weaker side is usually affected. The 2017 BPPV guideline recommends a supine roll test when the history fits but the Dix-Hallpike is negative.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-bppv': {
        "curveball": 'An elderly patient with posterior-canal BPPV is successfully repositioned. What does the 2017 guideline say about restrictions, imaging, medications and follow-up?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Do not impose post-maneuver postural restrictions, and do not routinely order imaging, vestibular testing or vestibular suppressants without another indication. Assess fall risk and home safety in older patients, counsel that recurrence is common, and reassess within about a month. Persistent symptoms prompt a search for the wrong canal, persistent BPPV, coexisting vestibular disease or a central disorder.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-csf-otorrhea-temporal-encephalocele': {
        "curveball": 'How do you confirm the fluid is CSF and map the tegmen defect before choosing a transmastoid versus a middle fossa repair?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Send the fluid for beta-2 transferrin or beta-trace protein. High-resolution temporal bone CT shows the bony defects, and T2 MRI separates herniated brain from fluid or effusion. Large or multiple defects, anteromedial defects or a sizable encephalocele favor a middle fossa or combined approach; small posterolateral tegmen mastoideum defects suit transmastoid repair. Also look for signs of idiopathic intracranial hypertension.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-csf-otorrhea-temporal-encephalocele': {
        "curveball": 'After a successful tegmen repair in this obese patient, what should you evaluate and treat to lower the chance of recurrence?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Spontaneous temporal bone leaks are strongly linked to idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Ask about headache, pulsatile tinnitus and visual change, get an ophthalmology exam for papilledema, and note imaging clues such as an empty sella. Opening pressure can be measured once the repair has healed. Treat with weight loss, acetazolamide and neurosurgical referral for shunting when pressure stays high. Check the other ear and skull base for further defects.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-central-vestibular-disorders': {
        "curveball": 'An emergency patient with acute vestibular syndrome has a normal MRI 6 hours after onset. Does that exclude stroke, and how do you decide?',
        "curveball_answer": 'No. Diffusion MRI misses a meaningful share of posterior circulation strokes in the first 24 to 48 hours, especially small ones. A HINTS exam (head impulse, nystagmus, test of skew) done by a trained examiner during ongoing continuous vertigo is more sensitive than early MRI. A central HINTS pattern or new hearing loss warrants admission, a stroke workup and repeat delayed MRI.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-central-vestibular-disorders': {
        "curveball": 'What if this patient also had sudden hearing loss in one ear and an abnormal head impulse? Does that make it peripheral?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Not necessarily. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery infarction can involve the labyrinth, producing acute hearing loss and an abnormal head impulse that mimic labyrinthitis. New hearing loss is the plus in HINTS plus, and it raises concern for stroke, especially with vascular risk factors. Arrange urgent neurology evaluation and MRI with vascular imaging rather than discharging on a peripheral diagnosis.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-chronic-otitis-media-cholesteatoma': {
        "curveball": 'When would you choose canal-wall-down over canal-wall-up mastoidectomy for this cholesteatoma, and how does that choice change follow-up?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Canal-wall-down suits extensive disease, a small sclerotic mastoid, recurrence after wall-up surgery, a large labyrinthine fistula, or a patient unlikely to return for a second look. It lowers residual and recurrent disease but leaves a cavity needing lifelong cleaning and water care. Canal-wall-up preserves anatomy but requires a planned second look or non-EPI diffusion-weighted MRI surveillance for residual cholesteatoma.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-chronic-otitis-media-cholesteatoma': {
        "curveball": 'At surgery you find cholesteatoma matrix stuck to a lateral semicircular canal fistula. How do you handle it?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Approaches vary with fistula size, hearing and surgical plan. A large fistula or an only-hearing ear favors leaving matrix in place and exteriorizing it in a canal-wall-down cavity. Otherwise, remove matrix at the end of the case, avoid suction over the opening, and seal immediately with fascia or bone pate; perioperative steroids are often given. Preoperative CT and a history of vertigo or a positive fistula test warn you in advance.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-cochlear-implant-candidacy': {
        "curveball": 'An adult has single-sided deafness with normal hearing in the other ear. Is cochlear implantation an option, and what counseling matters most?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Yes. The FDA approved cochlear implants for single-sided deafness in patients 5 and older in 2019. Unlike CROS aids or bone-conduction devices, which send sound to the hearing ear, an implant can restore some binaural hearing, improving localization and speech in noise and often reducing tinnitus. Counsel that benefit is better with shorter deafness, requires dedicated rehabilitation, and needs MRI confirmation of a cochlear nerve.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-cochlear-implant-candidacy': {
        "curveball": 'What imaging do you order for this post-meningitic child, and which finding would change the surgical plan?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Order high-resolution temporal bone CT plus MRI with heavily T2-weighted sequences. Early labyrinthitis ossificans is fibrous and can be missed on CT, while loss of cochlear fluid signal on T2 shows obliteration first. If the basal turn is obstructed, plan a drill-out, scala vestibuli insertion, a short or split array, or implant the less affected ear. Many teams implant both ears promptly before ossification progresses.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-cochlear-implant-failure-revision': {
        "curveball": 'What if the decline is from a scalp flap infection with an exposed receiver-stimulator? How do you manage it?',
        "curveball_answer": 'An exposed or biofilm-infected device rarely clears with antibiotics alone. Culture, cover Staphylococcus aureus including MRSA, and try debridement with rotation flap coverage when infection is limited. Persistent infection usually needs explantation; some surgeons cut the lead and leave the array, or place a dummy electrode, to keep the cochlear lumen open for reimplantation. Confirm pneumococcal vaccination.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-cochlear-implant-failure-revision': {
        "curveball": 'How do you counsel this patient about explantation and reimplantation for suspected soft failure, and what must be excluded first?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Soft failure is a diagnosis of exclusion: rule out programming problems, electrode migration or tip foldover on imaging, and medical causes such as infection or new middle-ear disease. Counsel that reimplantation usually restores or improves performance and often relieves painful or nonauditory sensations, but improvement is not guaranteed. The explanted device goes to the manufacturer for analysis, which may reveal a defect.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-cochlear-implant-surgery': {
        "curveball": 'You open the cochlea in a child with an incomplete partition malformation and get a CSF gusher. What do you do?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Raise the head, let the flow slow, and insert an electrode suited to malformations, often one with a stopper or cork, then pack the opening tightly with fascia or muscle. A lumbar drain is an option if flow persists. Anticipate gushers from preoperative CT and MRI, especially incomplete partition type III, and confirm array position with intraoperative imaging, because the array can enter the internal auditory canal.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-cochlear-implant-surgery': {
        "curveball": 'Despite a careful round-window insertion, the low-frequency hearing is gone a few weeks later. Why does delayed loss happen, and what measures try to prevent it?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Delayed loss reflects intracochlear inflammation, fibrosis and new bone around the array, along with hydrops and microtrauma. Common measures include perioperative systemic or topical steroids, avoiding open suction on perilymph, slow insertion through the round window, flexible lateral-wall arrays, and limiting insertion depth to the residual hearing. Counsel beforehand that preservation rates vary and combined electric-acoustic hearing may not be possible.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-congenital-inner-ear-malformations': {
        "curveball": 'A child with a malformed inner ear has an absent cochlear nerve on MRI. What device options remain, and how do you counsel the family?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A cochlear implant cannot stimulate an absent nerve, so an auditory brainstem implant on the cochlear nucleus becomes the option, at an experienced center. Outcomes are less predictable than with implants: many children gain sound awareness and some gain open-set speech. Keep visual language such as sign in parallel. Distinguish a truly absent nerve from a hypoplastic one, where a cochlear implant trial may still help.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-congenital-inner-ear-malformations': {
        "curveball": 'How would you counsel this family with enlarged vestibular aqueduct about activities, genetic testing and associated syndromes?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Advise avoiding contact sports and head trauma where practical; the evidence is limited but this counseling is standard. Offer SLC26A4 testing, since biallelic variants indicate Pendred syndrome, which can bring a goiter in adolescence, so monitor thyroid exam and function. Expect fluctuating, progressive loss: follow with serial audiograms, fit amplification, consider steroids for sudden drops, and plan cochlear implantation if hearing declines.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-cortical-neuroplasticity-in-hearing-loss': {
        "curveball": 'An adult who was born deaf and never used hearing aids requests a cochlear implant. How do you counsel expectations?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Long deprivation through the sensitive period lets auditory cortex be repurposed for other inputs such as vision. Prelingually deaf adults usually gain sound awareness and help with lipreading but limited open-set speech compared with postlingually deafened adults. Implantation can still be reasonable with realistic goals, motivation and some oral communication; prior aided hearing predicts better results.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-cortical-neuroplasticity-in-hearing-loss': {
        "curveball": 'For a profoundly deaf infant, how early can implantation be done, and what must be confirmed before surgery?',
        "curveball_answer": 'FDA labeling permits cochlear implantation from 9 months, and earlier surgery is done selectively, for example after meningitis. Before surgery confirm bilateral severe-to-profound loss with ABR and behavioral testing, limited benefit from a hearing-aid trial, imaging showing an implantable cochlea and cochlear nerve, anesthetic fitness and pneumococcal vaccination. Intensive auditory-verbal rehabilitation must follow.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-eac-exostoses-osteoma-canalplasty': {
        "curveball": 'When is canalplasty indicated for exostoses, and which structures are most at risk while drilling?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Operate when the canal is nearly occluded and causes recurrent otitis externa, trapped keratin or conductive loss despite conservative care. Preserve canal skin flaps. Drilling posteriorly near the annulus endangers the mastoid segment of the facial nerve; anteriorly you can enter the temporomandibular joint; medially protect the tympanic membrane and ossicles. Cold-water exposure must stop afterward or the lesions regrow.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-eac-exostoses-osteoma-canalplasty': {
        "curveball": 'How would you tell these exostoses from an osteoma on exam and CT, and does the distinction change the operation?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Exostoses are multiple, bilateral, broad-based and medial, near the annulus, and linked to cold-water exposure. Osteomas are solitary, unilateral and often pedunculated, arising more laterally near the tympanosquamous or tympanomastoid suture, with fibrovascular channels on histology. An osteoma can often be removed by drilling through its narrow pedicle, while exostoses need a wider canalplasty with skin flaps. Both are observed when asymptomatic.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-eustachian-tube-dysfunction': {
        "curveball": 'When is balloon dilation of the Eustachian tube appropriate, and what must be excluded before offering it?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Consider it for adults with persistent obstructive dysfunction, typically symptoms for at least 3 months with objective support such as abnormal tympanometry or retraction, after medical therapy fails. Exclude patulous Eustachian tube, which dilation would worsen, and other causes of fullness: temporomandibular disorders, sudden sensorineural loss, superior canal dehiscence and nasopharyngeal pathology. Review imaging for carotid canal dehiscence when available.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-eustachian-tube-dysfunction': {
        "curveball": 'Nasopharyngoscopy shows fullness in the fossa of Rosenmuller. What is your next step, and which blood test helps?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Biopsy the lesion endoscopically to evaluate for nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and obtain MRI of the nasopharynx, skull base and neck for staging under AJCC Version 9. Plasma EBV DNA supports diagnosis, staging and surveillance, especially in nonkeratinizing disease. If biopsy is negative but suspicion persists, repeat a deeper or MRI-guided biopsy. Locoregionally advanced disease is treated with chemoradiation.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-external-auditory-canal-cholesteatoma': {
        "curveball": 'In an elderly patient, how do you tell canal cholesteatoma from keratosis obturans and from carcinoma?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Canal cholesteatoma is usually unilateral in older patients, with dull otalgia, otorrhea and focal erosion of the inferior or posterior bony canal, often with sequestrum. Keratosis obturans tends to be bilateral in younger patients, with acute severe pain, conductive loss and circumferential widening. Persistent granulation, bleeding or pain out of proportion calls for biopsy to exclude squamous cell carcinoma, and diabetes raises necrotizing otitis externa.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-external-auditory-canal-cholesteatoma': {
        "curveball": 'What if CT shows the erosion extends into the mastoid and approaches the facial nerve? How does management change?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Disease beyond the canal wall needs surgery rather than office debridement. Excise the matrix and diseased bone through a canalplasty or mastoidectomy, then reconstruct the canal with fascia, cartilage or a skin graft. Use facial nerve monitoring and identify the mastoid segment early when erosion approaches it. Send tissue to pathology to exclude carcinoma, and follow with microscopy for recurrence.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-facial-nerve-schwannoma': {
        "curveball": "During surgery for presumed cholesteatoma you unexpectedly find a facial nerve schwannoma, and the patient's face is normal. What do you do?",
        "curveball_answer": 'Do not resect it. Resection usually means sacrificing the nerve and grafting, which at best leaves House-Brackmann III function, and the patient has not consented to that. Limit yourself to confirming the diagnosis, consider bony decompression only if safe, close, and obtain MRI. Then counsel about observation, decompression or radiosurgery, reserving resection with grafting for worsening function.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-facial-nerve-schwannoma': {
        "curveball": "When does this patient's facial function fall enough to favor resection with grafting, and what result should you promise?",
        "curveball_answer": 'Many surgeons offer resection once weakness reaches about House-Brackmann III to IV, or when growth threatens hearing or the brainstem, because grafting works better before prolonged severe paralysis. Resection with a great auricular or sural interposition graft typically yields House-Brackmann III at best, with synkinesis. Promise tone and eye closure, not normal movement, and plan eye care and later facial rehabilitation.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-facial-paralysis': {
        "curveball": 'A patient diagnosed with Bell palsy has no recovery at 4 months. What do you do next?',
        "curveball_answer": 'The 2013 Bell palsy guideline calls for reassessment or referral when recovery is incomplete 3 months after onset, or with new neurologic findings or eye symptoms. Obtain contrast MRI covering the facial nerve course from brainstem through temporal bone and parotid to look for tumor, and examine the parotid and facial skin. Protect the eye and plan rehabilitation and reanimation based on duration and electromyography.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-facial-paralysis': {
        "curveball": "What if this patient's forehead were spared while the lower face was paralyzed? Where is the lesion, and what is the next step?",
        "curveball_answer": 'Forehead sparing points to an upper motor neuron lesion above the facial nucleus, because upper facial motor neurons receive bilateral cortical input. This is not Bell palsy. Treat it as a possible stroke or intracranial mass, with urgent neurologic assessment and brain imaging. Partial peripheral lesions can occasionally mimic sparing, so examine carefully, but never give steroids and send home without excluding a central cause.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-glomus-tumor-paraganglioma': {
        "curveball": 'What if CT shows the mass sits on the promontory with an intact jugular plate? How does that change management?',
        "curveball_answer": 'An intact jugular plate with a mass limited to the middle ear indicates a glomus tympanicum rather than a jugulare. These are usually removed through a transcanal or postauricular tympanotomy with low morbidity, and preoperative embolization is generally unnecessary. Observation or radiation is reasonable in elderly or poor surgical candidates. Screening for catecholamine secretion and SDHx variants still applies.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-glomus-tumor-paraganglioma': {
        "curveball": 'Before embolizing this Fisch C tumor, which laboratory test must you check, and why?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Measure plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines. A secreting paraganglioma or an unsuspected pheochromocytoma can cause hypertensive crisis during embolization, anesthesia or tumor handling, so secreting tumors need alpha-blockade before any beta-blockade. Genetic testing for SDHx variants matters too, since multifocal carotid or vagal paragangliomas change surgical planning and lower cranial nerve risk.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-gradenigo-syndrome-petrous-apicitis': {
        "curveball": 'Despite IV antibiotics and a tube, the child still has fever and an abducens palsy at 72 hours, and MRI shows a petrous apex abscess. What next?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Failure of medical therapy with an abscess needs surgical drainage: mastoidectomy following pneumatized cell tracts to the apex, such as infralabyrinthine or subarcuate routes, or an endoscopic endonasal approach for medial lesions abutting the sphenoid. Look for venous sinus thrombosis, meningitis and epidural abscess with MRI and MRV, and involve neurosurgery and infectious disease.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-gradenigo-syndrome-petrous-apicitis': {
        "curveball": 'Why does trigeminal pain accompany the abducens palsy here, and what other deficits can appear as inflammation spreads?',
        "curveball_answer": 'The petrous apex sits against Meckel cave and the trigeminal ganglion, so inflammation causes retro-orbital V1 pain. The abducens nerve runs under the petroclinoid (Gruber) ligament in Dorello canal, where it is tethered and vulnerable. Spread can affect the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves, and cavernous sinus thrombosis can add oculomotor and trochlear palsies, signaling a more dangerous intracranial complication.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-hearing-aids-and-bone-conduction-devices': {
        "curveball": 'A patient with chronically draining ears and bilateral mixed loss cannot tolerate air-conduction aids. Which device, and what determines candidacy?',
        "curveball_answer": "A bone-conduction device bypasses the canal and middle ear. Candidacy depends on cochlear reserve: bone-conduction thresholds must fall within the chosen device's fitting range, with more powerful devices accepting poorer reserve. Trial a softband or headband first to show benefit. Percutaneous abutments give strong output but risk skin reactions; transcutaneous devices reduce skin problems but can limit high-frequency gain.",
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-hearing-aids-and-bone-conduction-devices': {
        "curveball": 'Could a cochlear implant restore localization in this patient instead, and does his schwannoma surgery affect that option?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A cochlear implant in a deaf ear can restore some binaural hearing and localization, which CROS aids and bone-conduction devices cannot. But it needs a functioning cochlear nerve. After translabyrinthine or most standard resections the cochlear nerve is cut or nonfunctional, so implantation is possible only when the nerve was anatomically and functionally preserved, which must be confirmed before surgery.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-hyperacusis-decreased-sound-tolerance': {
        "curveball": "What if the patient's intolerance is triggered only by chewing and breathing sounds made by family members?",
        "curveball_answer": 'That is misophonia: an emotional reaction, often anger or disgust, to specific trigger sounds, usually made by people, rather than a loudness problem. Audiometry and loudness discomfort levels are often normal. Management centers on cognitive behavioral therapy and sound-masking strategies, often with psychology, rather than general sound desensitization. Screen for anxiety and obsessive-compulsive features.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-hyperacusis-decreased-sound-tolerance': {
        "curveball": 'Which otologic conditions can present with sound sensitivity like hers and must be excluded before blaming central gain?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Exclude superior canal dehiscence and other third-window lesions, with sound-induced vertigo and autophony; facial paralysis such as Bell palsy or Ramsay Hunt, where stapedius loss makes sound uncomfortably loud; recruitment from cochlear loss; Meniere disease; perilymph fistula; and migraine with phonophobia. Obtain audiometry with acoustic reflexes and loudness discomfort levels, and image only when findings suggest a structural cause.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-keratosis-obturans': {
        "curveball": 'Despite careful removal and drops, the plugs recur every few weeks. What is your next step?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Recurrence is expected because the epithelial migration defect persists. Intensify maintenance with scheduled microscopic cleaning, regular softening drops and intermittent topical steroids for inflammation. Reconsider the diagnosis: focal bone erosion suggests canal cholesteatoma, and a mass or granulation needs biopsy. CT helps if bone changes are suspected. Canalplasty is reserved for rare refractory cases with bony narrowing.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-keratosis-obturans': {
        "curveball": 'Which systemic associations of bilateral keratosis obturans should you look for in a young patient like this, and why?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Keratosis obturans in younger patients is classically associated with bronchiectasis and chronic rhinosinusitis, suggesting a shared defect in epithelial or mucociliary clearance. With respiratory disease present, ask about chronic cough and sinus symptoms and consider primary ciliary dyskinesia or cystic fibrosis evaluation with pulmonology. Treat the ear with careful debridement, topical steroids and regular cleaning.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-labyrinthitis-infections-of-the-labyrinth': {
        "curveball": 'How do you treat viral labyrinthitis with sudden hearing loss differently from bacterial labyrinthitis complicating otitis media?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Viral labyrinthitis with sudden sensorineural loss follows the sudden hearing loss pathway: prompt audiometry, consideration of oral or intratympanic steroids, short-term vestibular suppressants and early rehabilitation. Bacterial labyrinthitis from otitis media or cholesteatoma needs IV antibiotics, myringotomy for culture and drainage, imaging for intracranial complications, and mastoidectomy for cholesteatoma or coalescent disease.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-labyrinthitis-infections-of-the-labyrinth': {
        "curveball": 'Months after suppurative labyrinthitis the patient has no vertigo but the ear is dead. Why, and what rehabilitation fits?',
        "curveball_answer": 'The labyrinth was destroyed, leaving a stable unilateral loss that the brain compensates for, so there is no ongoing asymmetry to cause vertigo. Vestibular therapy addresses residual imbalance. For the deaf ear consider CROS, a bone-conduction device or a cochlear implant for single-sided deafness, but check CT and T2 MRI for labyrinthitis ossificans, which can block electrode insertion if implantation is delayed.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-lateral-skull-base-tumor-framework': {
        "curveball": 'A CPA mass is bright on diffusion-weighted MRI and does not enhance. What is it, and how does management differ from a schwannoma?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Restricted diffusion without enhancement means an epidermoid cyst, which insinuates around nerves and vessels rather than displacing them; an arachnoid cyst follows CSF on every sequence, including diffusion. Epidermoids are removed when symptomatic, but capsule stuck to nerves and vessels is often left to avoid deficits, accepting slow recurrence. Spilled contents can cause chemical meningitis.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-lateral-skull-base-tumor-framework': {
        "curveball": 'How would a vestibular schwannoma look different on MRI, and why does the distinction matter for surgical planning?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A vestibular schwannoma is centered on the porus, enters and widens the internal auditory canal, meets the petrous bone at an acute angle in an ice-cream-cone shape, and may be cystic without a dural tail. Meningiomas often spare the canal and may invade bone. The diagnosis changes the expected nerve position, approach choice, hearing preservation chances and the need to resect involved dura.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-m-ni-re-disease': {
        "curveball": 'A Meniere patient with serviceable hearing still has disabling vertigo after medical therapy and intratympanic steroids. What are the options, and which spare hearing?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Under the 2020 Meniere guideline, next options are intratympanic gentamicin, which carries some hearing risk; endolymphatic sac surgery, which is nondestructive; and vestibular nerve section, which preserves hearing but is intracranial. Labyrinthectomy is reserved for nonserviceable hearing. Before any destructive step, confirm the affected side and normal vestibular function in the other ear, since disease can become bilateral.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-m-ni-re-disease': {
        "curveball": 'Which treatments does the 2020 Meniere guideline recommend against, and what lifestyle and rehabilitation advice does it support?',
        "curveball_answer": 'It recommends against positive pressure therapy, and against ordering vestibular function testing or electrocochleography simply to establish the diagnosis. Diuretics or betahistine may be offered for maintenance as options. Clinicians should educate about dietary and lifestyle changes such as limiting salt, caffeine and alcohol, offer vestibular rehabilitation for chronic imbalance between attacks, and counsel about amplification for hearing loss.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-necrotizing-otitis-externa': {
        "curveball": 'Which imaging best defines soft-tissue spread in suspected necrotizing otitis externa, and how do you judge response to treatment?',
        "curveball_answer": 'CT shows bone erosion but lags behind disease and stays abnormal after cure. MRI better shows soft-tissue and marrow spread toward the skull base and nasopharynx. Technetium bone scan is sensitive but not specific, so gallium, labeled leukocyte scans or PET-CT are used to follow activity. Falling ESR or CRP and resolving pain guide the length of antibiotics, usually at least 6 weeks.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-necrotizing-otitis-externa': {
        "curveball": 'Cultures grow no Pseudomonas, but the biopsy shows fungal hyphae. What does that mean, and what else must the biopsy exclude?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Fungal necrotizing otitis externa, often Aspergillus, occurs in diabetic or immunocompromised patients who fail antipseudomonal therapy; treat with voriconazole and debridement as needed. Biopsy of granulation is essential before prolonged therapy because squamous cell carcinoma can mimic the disease. Prior antibiotic drops can also make cultures negative, so interpret a negative bacterial culture cautiously.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-neurotologic-intraoperative-cranial-nerve-monitoring': {
        "curveball": 'What if the facial EMG response disappears completely after a traction moment? Do you abandon the resection?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Pause dissection, release retraction, irrigate with warm saline, and check the system: electrodes, stimulator, and that anesthesia has not given a paralytic. Stimulate proximally at the brainstem. If responses return, continue gently, accepting residual tumor on the nerve if needed. If they stay lost, many surgeons stop and leave tumor behind, especially in older patients, since protecting function usually outweighs complete removal.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-neurotologic-intraoperative-cranial-nerve-monitoring': {
        "curveball": 'If the nerve were anatomically intact but needed a much higher current at the brainstem to respond, how would you counsel the patient?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A high proximal threshold, or a low proximal-to-distal amplitude ratio, predicts a greater chance of early postoperative weakness. Because the nerve is intact, most patients still improve over months, though recovery may be incomplete. Protect the eye, consider early eyelid weighting if closure is poor, follow closely, and consider reanimation only if there is no recovery after about a year.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-ossicular-discontinuity': {
        "curveball": 'At exploration you find an eroded incus long process with a mobile, intact stapes. How do you reconstruct?',
        "curveball_answer": "Options include incus interposition, sculpting the patient's incus to bridge the malleus and stapes head; a partial ossicular replacement prosthesis onto the stapes capitulum; or bone cement for a small long-process gap. Place cartilage between any prosthesis and the drum to prevent extrusion. An intact, mobile stapes superstructure predicts better hearing than total reconstruction to the footplate.",
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-ossicular-discontinuity': {
        "curveball": 'What if this patient also had vertigo and falling bone-conduction thresholds after the injury? How do urgency and surgical plan change?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Vertigo or worsening sensorineural loss after trauma suggests stapes subluxation into the vestibule or perilymph fistula, not simple incudostapedial separation. Obtain CT looking for pneumolabyrinth and a displaced stapes, and consider early exploration to seal the oval window with tissue. Avoid manipulating a fractured footplate; ossicular reconstruction can be staged once the inner ear is stable.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-otologic-manifestations-of-systemic-disease': {
        "curveball": 'Which systemic causes of progressive or fluctuating sensorineural loss are treatable, and which tests would you order?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Treatable causes include syphilis, autoimmune disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis, lupus or Cogan syndrome, sarcoidosis, Lyme disease, hypothyroidism and Paget disease. Guided by history, order treponemal serology, and when suggested ANCA, ANA, ESR or CRP and thyroid function. Otosyphilis is treated like neurosyphilis with IV penicillin, often with corticosteroids.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-otologic-manifestations-of-systemic-disease': {
        "curveball": "Once granulomatosis with polyangiitis is confirmed, how would you manage this patient's ears and hearing?",
        "curveball_answer": 'Systemic induction with glucocorticoids plus rituximab or cyclophosphamide, directed by rheumatology, treats the ear disease, and hearing loss, including sensorineural loss, can improve with prompt immunosuppression. Tubes in active disease often drain chronically, so avoid unnecessary otologic surgery and use hearing aids. Watch for facial palsy, mastoiditis and pachymeningitis, which signal active disease.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-otosclerosis-stapes-fixation': {
        "curveball": 'What are the main contraindications to stapedotomy, and how do you counsel about its most feared complication?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Avoid surgery in an only-hearing ear, with active infection or a perforation, during active Meniere disease, and in patients unfit for surgery; operate the worse ear first. The feared complication is profound sensorineural loss, a dead ear, in about 1 percent. Also counsel about vertigo, taste change from chorda tympani injury, facial nerve injury, perilymph fistula and later prosthesis displacement.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-otosclerosis-stapes-fixation': {
        "curveball": 'What if the loss became far-advanced, with severe cochlear involvement beyond what a stapedotomy alone can fix? What options remain?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Far-advanced otosclerosis may still benefit from stapedotomy plus a powerful hearing aid. When aided speech is inadequate, cochlear implantation works well. CT may show retrofenestral cochlear otosclerosis with spongiotic bone, which raises the risk of facial nerve stimulation and difficult insertion. Sodium fluoride has been used for cochlear otosclerosis, but evidence is limited.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-ototoxic-noise-induced-hearing-loss': {
        "curveball": 'In a child receiving cisplatin for a localized solid tumor, which drug reduces ototoxicity, and how should hearing be monitored?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Sodium thiosulfate is FDA approved to reduce cisplatin ototoxicity in children with localized, nonmetastatic solid tumors; it is given about 6 hours after cisplatin so it does not blunt antitumor effect. Obtain baseline and serial audiometry including extended high frequencies, use OAEs or ABR in young children, and grade changes with a standard scale so oncology can weigh dose changes.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-patulous-eustachian-tube-dysfunction': {
        "curveball": 'What is first-line conservative management, and what features would make you suspect superior canal dehiscence instead?',
        "curveball_answer": "Reassure, hydrate, reverse recent weight loss where possible, stop decongestants, and try saline nasal sprays; other topical agents have limited evidence. Hearing one's breathing that resolves when supine favors patulous tube. Hearing eye movements, footsteps or pulse, sound-induced vertigo, and a low-frequency conductive gap with bone conduction better than 0 dB and present reflexes favor dehiscence; CT and VEMP sort these out.",
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-patulous-eustachian-tube-dysfunction': {
        "curveball": 'Conservative measures fail for this patient. What procedural options exist, and what complications do you warn about?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Options include a tympanostomy tube to damp drum excursion, patulous tube plugs passed through the drum, augmentation of the tubal orifice with fat, cartilage or filler, and surgical reconstruction of the torus. Warn that overcorrection can convert patulous to obstructive dysfunction with effusion, results are variable, and serious vascular complications have been reported with injected fillers near the tube.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-perilymph-fistula-inner-ear-window-leak': {
        "curveball": 'After a cotton-swab injury, a patient has vertigo, worsening sensorineural loss and a stapes driven into the vestibule. What is the urgency and approach?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Penetrating trauma with a displaced stapes, progressive sensorineural loss or persistent vertigo warrants urgent exploration rather than observation. CT showing pneumolabyrinth supports a fistula. At tympanotomy, avoid pulling a displaced stapes out of the vestibule; reposition it gently or remove only what is needed, seal the oval window with tissue, and stage ossicular reconstruction.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-perilymph-fistula-inner-ear-window-leak': {
        "curveball": 'Which other diving injury must you distinguish from inner-ear barotrauma in this diver, and why does it matter?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Inner-ear decompression sickness typically occurs during or after ascent, often with other decompression symptoms, and needs urgent hyperbaric recompression. Inner-ear barotrauma follows difficult descent or forceful Valsalva, and recompression could worsen a window rupture, so it is managed with bed rest, head elevation and avoiding straining. Return to diving after proven barotrauma with lasting loss is often discouraged.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-persistent-postural-perceptual-dizziness-pppd': {
        "curveball": 'Which medication is commonly tried for PPPD, how long until benefit, and what counseling helps the patient stick with it?',
        "curveball_answer": 'SSRIs or SNRIs such as sertraline or venlafaxine are commonly used, started at low doses because these patients are sensitive to side effects, with benefit appearing over several weeks. Explain that PPPD is a real functional disorder of balance processing, that the drug helps even without depression, and that it works best combined with graded vestibular rehabilitation and cognitive behavioral therapy.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-persistent-postural-perceptual-dizziness-pppd': {
        "curveball": 'Which Barany Society criteria must she meet to be diagnosed with PPPD, and which finding would argue against it?',
        "curveball_answer": 'PPPD requires non-spinning dizziness or unsteadiness on most days for at least 3 months, worsened by upright posture, active or passive motion, and complex visual stimuli, usually after a precipitating event, and causing distress or impairment. Spontaneous or positional nystagmus, focal neurologic signs, or new uncompensated vestibular loss on testing point to another diagnosis that must be treated first.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-petrous-apex-lesions': {
        "curveball": 'An incidental T1-bright petrous apex lesion is not expansile and loses signal with fat suppression. What is it, and does it need follow-up?',
        "curveball_answer": 'This is asymmetric fatty marrow in a nonpneumatized apex, a normal variant. CT shows intact trabecular bone without expansion, and fat-suppressed images confirm fat. No treatment or follow-up is needed. Do not confuse it with cholesterol granuloma, which is bright on T1 and T2, does not suppress with fat saturation, and expands bone.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-petrous-apex-lesions': {
        "curveball": 'This cholesterol granuloma now causes headache and an abducens palsy. What are the surgical options, and what anatomy guides the route?',
        "curveball_answer": "Symptomatic cholesterol granulomas are drained with a lasting drainage pathway rather than excised. An endoscopic endonasal transsphenoidal route is favored when the lesion abuts the sphenoid, often with a stent or flap to keep it open. Infracochlear or infralabyrinthine routes preserve hearing, and middle fossa approaches are used selectively. The petrous carotid's position relative to the cyst on CT drives the choice.",
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-ramsay-hunt-syndrome-herpes-zoster-oticus': {
        "curveball": 'What if a patient has an acute facial palsy with no rash at all, but VZV is later confirmed? What is that called, and does treatment change?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Zoster sine herpete: VZV reactivation without vesicles, confirmed by rising antibody titers or PCR. It behaves like Ramsay Hunt syndrome, with a worse prognosis than Bell palsy. Treat with corticosteroids plus an antiviral such as valacyclovir, protect the eye, and obtain audiometry. It is often first labeled Bell palsy, so check the concha, canal and oropharynx carefully for subtle vesicles.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-ramsay-hunt-syndrome-herpes-zoster-oticus': {
        "curveball": 'How do you counsel this patient about hearing and balance recovery, and what follow-up would you arrange?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Hearing loss recovers in some patients, more often when it is mild, but severe loss often persists. Vertigo usually improves through central compensation, helped by vestibular rehabilitation. Arrange baseline and follow-up audiometry, continued eye care, pain control including watching for postherpetic neuralgia, hearing aids if needed, and facial rehabilitation if recovery is incomplete.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-sudden-sensorineural-hearing-loss': {
        "curveball": 'Recovery is incomplete three weeks after oral steroids. What does the 2019 guideline recommend next, and what imaging?',
        "curveball_answer": 'The 2019 sudden hearing loss guideline recommends offering intratympanic steroid as salvage therapy for incomplete recovery 2 to 6 weeks after onset. Evaluate for retrocochlear pathology with gadolinium MRI of the internal auditory canals, or ABR if MRI is not possible, and do not order routine head CT. Repeat audiometry after treatment and within 6 months, and counsel about hearing aids and assistive devices.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-sudden-sensorineural-hearing-loss': {
        "curveball": 'What if this patient has poorly controlled diabetes? How would you tailor steroid therapy?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Diabetes is a relative, not absolute, contraindication to systemic steroids. Intratympanic steroid can be used as initial therapy and avoids systemic hyperglycemia; the guideline allows it when systemic steroids are risky. Alternatively, give oral steroids with close glucose monitoring coordinated with the primary physician. Either way, start promptly, since treatment within 2 weeks of onset gives the best chance of benefit.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-superior-canal-dehiscence': {
        "curveball": 'CT shows a dehiscent superior canal, but the patient has no symptoms. Do you operate?',
        "curveball_answer": "No. Radiographic dehiscence on CT is far more common than the clinical syndrome, and partial volume averaging overcalls thin bone. Diagnosis needs symptoms plus physiologic confirmation, such as low cervical VEMP thresholds or enlarged ocular VEMP amplitudes, a low-frequency conductive gap with bone conduction better than 0 dB, or sound- or pressure-evoked nystagmus in the canal's plane. Asymptomatic patients are observed.",
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-superior-canal-dehiscence': {
        "curveball": 'This patient has disabling symptoms. Which surgical approach and repair would you choose, and what is the main risk to hearing?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A middle fossa craniotomy gives direct exposure for plugging or resurfacing; a transmastoid approach avoids temporal lobe retraction and suits many defects. Plugging is generally more durable than resurfacing alone. The main risk is sensorineural loss, higher in revision surgery and when the other ear is also affected, plus temporary vestibular hypofunction. Bilateral dehiscence is common, so check the other side.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-temporal-bone-anatomy': {
        "curveball": 'Which three structures bound the facial recess, and which one might you deliberately sacrifice if the recess is very narrow?',
        "curveball_answer": 'The facial recess is bounded medially by the mastoid segment of the facial nerve, laterally by the chorda tympani, and superiorly by the incus buttress. In a narrow recess the chorda may be sacrificed to widen access, after counseling about taste change. The facial nerve is never sacrificed: thin the bone over it with a diamond burr under continuous irrigation, using monitoring as an adjunct.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-temporal-bone-fracture': {
        "curveball": 'A patient develops facial weakness on day three after a temporal bone fracture. How does that differ from immediate complete paralysis?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Delayed-onset or incomplete paralysis implies edema with an intact nerve and carries an excellent prognosis with observation and corticosteroids if safe. Immediate complete paralysis suggests transection or bony impingement: obtain CT and electroneurography between days 3 and 14, and consider exploration and decompression when degeneration reaches 90 percent or more and no voluntary EMG activity is present.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-temporal-bone-fracture': {
        "curveball": 'What late complication makes this fracture pattern need long-term counseling, and which preventive measure should you offer?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Otic capsule-violating fractures heal with fibrous tissue rather than bone, leaving a lasting pathway for infection, so meningitis can occur years later. Offer pneumococcal vaccination, counsel prompt evaluation of fever, headache or ear infection, and repair any persistent CSF leak. The ear is usually deaf; if implantation is considered, evaluate early before the cochlea ossifies.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-tinnitus': {
        "curveball": 'A patient has unilateral pulsatile tinnitus and a normal otoscopic exam. What is the workup, and which diagnosis must not be missed?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Unilateral pulsatile tinnitus warrants imaging. CT angiography or temporal bone CT shows paraganglioma, dehiscent or high jugular bulb, sigmoid sinus diverticulum or dehiscence, and aberrant carotid; MRI with MRA and MRV assesses dural arteriovenous fistula, idiopathic intracranial hypertension and venous sinus stenosis. A dural arteriovenous fistula must not be missed because cortical venous drainage can lead to hemorrhage.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-tinnitus': {
        "curveball": "How would you treat this patient's bothersome tinnitus, and which treatments should you advise against?",
        "curveball_answer": 'Fit hearing aids for the measurable loss, which often reduces tinnitus awareness, and offer cognitive behavioral therapy, which has the best evidence for reducing distress, plus education and sound therapy. Advise against antidepressants, anticonvulsants, anxiolytics or intratympanic drugs as routine tinnitus treatment, and against supplements such as ginkgo, melatonin or zinc. Treat comorbid insomnia, anxiety or depression on their own merits.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-tympanic-membrane-perforation': {
        "curveball": 'What if the chronic perforation is marginal and anterior? What must you exclude, and how does it affect grafting?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A marginal perforation lacks an annular rim, so squamous epithelium can migrate into the middle ear: look carefully for cholesteatoma with the microscope and consider CT. Anterior perforations are the hardest to graft because of poor exposure and blood supply. Anterior tucking of an underlay graft, cartilage grafts or a lateral graft improve take, and an overhanging anterior canal wall may need canalplasty.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-tympanic-membrane-perforation': {
        "curveball": 'What if the slap injury had also caused vertigo and a sensorineural drop? How would your management change?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Vertigo or sensorineural loss after a pressure injury raises concern for perilymph fistula or stapes subluxation rather than a simple perforation. Obtain urgent audiometry and CT, and consider early exploration with oval-window sealing if signs persist or progress. Keep the ear dry and avoid straining or nose blowing. A perforation without inner-ear signs can still be observed.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-tympanometry-acoustic-reflexes': {
        "curveball": 'A flat tympanogram comes back with an ear canal volume of 0.2 mL. What does that mean, and what should you do?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A very small volume with a flat tracing means the probe is not measuring the middle ear: it is blocked by cerumen or debris, pressed against the canal wall, or the canal is occluded. Look in the ear, clean it, reposition the probe and repeat the test. If the canal is clear, consider stenosis or a mass. Never diagnose effusion from this tracing.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-tympanometry-acoustic-reflexes': {
        "curveball": 'In the other ear, which had a tube placed last month, the tympanogram is flat with normal canal volume. What does that mean, and what do you do?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A flat tracing with normal canal volume in an ear with a tube means the tube is not patent: blocked by dried blood, mucus or granulation, or extruded with fluid behind an intact drum. Examine with the microscope. A blocked tube is usually treated first with antibiotic-steroid drops or office cleaning; replace it only if effusion and symptoms persist.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-vernet-syndrome-jugular-foramen-syndrome': {
        "curveball": 'This patient with jugular foramen syndrome is aspirating. How do you evaluate the swallow, and which interventions reduce aspiration?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Combined IX and X loss causes pharyngeal weakness and glottic insufficiency. Obtain an instrumental swallow study such as FEES or modified barium swallow, plus laryngoscopy. Injection or framework medialization of the paralyzed fold improves cough and airway protection, and swallow therapy teaches compensatory maneuvers; cricopharyngeal procedures help selected patients. Older patients compensate poorly, which weighs against surgery that could add new deficits.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-vernet-syndrome-jugular-foramen-syndrome': {
        "curveball": 'What if this patient also had tongue deviation and an ipsilateral Horner syndrome? Where would you now localize the lesion?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Adding CN XII and the sympathetic chain places the lesion below the skull base in the retrostyloid parapharyngeal space, Villaret syndrome, rather than in the jugular foramen alone; Collet-Sicard syndrome is IX to XII without Horner. Image the skull base and neck with MRI and contrast CT to look for paraganglioma, schwannoma, metastasis or nodal disease, and consider carotid dissection with vascular imaging.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-vestibular-migraine': {
        "curveball": 'Lifestyle measures fail in this vestibular migraine patient. Which preventive medications are reasonable, and how do comorbidities guide the choice?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Preventives are borrowed from migraine: propranolol, tricyclics such as amitriptyline or nortriptyline, verapamil, topiramate, venlafaxine and CGRP-targeted agents. Choose by comorbidity: avoid propranolol in asthma, favor amitriptyline with insomnia, and consider topiramate with obesity but not with kidney stones. Evidence specific to vestibular migraine is limited, so set a trial period and involve neurology for refractory cases.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-vestibular-migraine': {
        "curveball": 'What if serial audiograms later show fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss in one ear? How do you handle the overlap?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Vestibular migraine and Meniere disease overlap and can coexist. Documented fluctuating low-frequency loss meeting criteria supports adding Meniere disease. Treat both, with migraine prevention plus Meniere measures. Hold off on destructive treatments until vertigo is clearly ear-driven, because migraine-driven attacks will not respond and the patient could lose hearing or balance function for no benefit.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-vestibular-neuritis': {
        "curveball": 'Should this vestibular neuritis patient receive corticosteroids or antivirals, and how strong is the evidence?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Early corticosteroids may speed recovery of caloric function, but evidence for long-term symptom benefit is mixed, so they are an option rather than a firm standard. Antivirals add no benefit. Whatever the choice, the key steps are excluding stroke, limiting suppressants to the first few days, mobilizing early and starting vestibular rehabilitation.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-vestibular-neuritis': {
        "curveball": 'Weeks after recovering from vestibular neuritis, the patient develops brief vertigo when rolling over in bed. What is the likely cause?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Posterior canal BPPV is a well-known sequel of superior vestibular neuritis. Utricular damage can release otoconia, while the posterior canal, supplied by the spared inferior vestibular nerve, still works and can signal the debris. Perform a Dix-Hallpike test and treat with a canalith repositioning maneuver such as the Epley. This is distinct from incomplete compensation and responds quickly.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-vestibular-rehabilitation': {
        "curveball": 'A patient with bilateral vestibular loss from gentamicin has oscillopsia when walking. How does rehabilitation differ from unilateral loss?',
        "curveball_answer": 'With little residual function to adapt, therapy emphasizes substitution: using vision, somatosensory cues and the cervico-ocular reflex, along with gait and balance training. Add fall-prevention, home safety review, and advice to avoid walking in the dark or on uneven ground. Gaze-stabilization exercises help less than in unilateral loss, and recovery is often partial; vestibular implants remain investigational.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-vestibular-rehabilitation': {
        "curveball": 'Which specific exercises would you prescribe for her, and what recovery timeline would make you re-evaluate?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Prescribe gaze-stabilization exercises, keeping the eyes on a target while moving the head, habituation to provoking movements, and progressive balance and gait training, done several times daily at home. Most patients improve over weeks to a few months. Persistent symptoms beyond about 3 months warrant re-evaluation for incomplete compensation, superimposed BPPV, PPPD or a missed central cause.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-vestibular-schwannoma': {
        "curveball": 'A 72-year-old has a small intracanalicular vestibular schwannoma with serviceable hearing. What is the default plan, and what triggers treatment?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Wait and scan: repeat MRI at about 6 months, then yearly if stable, because many small tumors grow slowly or not at all. Treatment, often stereotactic radiosurgery in an older patient, is triggered by documented growth, new or worsening symptoms, or patient preference. Counsel that hearing can decline even without growth and that no option guarantees hearing preservation.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-vestibular-schwannoma': {
        "curveball": 'What if the tumor were 3.5 cm, compressing the brainstem, with hydrocephalus? How does management change?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Large tumors with brainstem compression are managed surgically, since radiosurgery is poorly suited to large tumors with mass effect and can cause transient swelling. Hydrocephalus may need CSF diversion before or with resection. Translabyrinthine or retrosigmoid approaches are used, and planned subtotal resection with later radiosurgery for the remnant is increasingly chosen to protect facial function.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-vestibular-test-battery': {
        "curveball": 'Before intratympanic gentamicin for Meniere disease, what testing do you need, and why does the other ear matter?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Confirm normal vestibular function in the other ear with calorics and video head impulse testing, plus audiometry. Meniere disease can become bilateral, and ablating the only working labyrinth causes bilateral vestibular loss with permanent oscillopsia and imbalance. Baseline testing of the treated ear also documents function so the effect of gentamicin can be measured.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-vestibular-test-battery': {
        "curveball": 'Which test would assess the saccule and inferior vestibular nerve, which lateral-canal calorics and head impulse testing miss?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Cervical VEMP tests the saccule and inferior vestibular nerve, while ocular VEMP tests the utricle and superior vestibular nerve. Video head impulse testing can also check the posterior canal, supplied by the inferior nerve, and the anterior canal, supplied by the superior nerve. Calorics test only the lateral canal at low frequency. Together these separate superior from inferior vestibular neuritis.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-aom-ome-tympanostomy-decisions': {
        "curveball": 'A child has bilateral effusions for 4 months with documented hearing difficulty and a speech delay. What does the 2022 tubes guideline recommend?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Offer bilateral tympanostomy tubes: the 2022 guideline recommends them for bilateral effusion lasting 3 months or longer with documented hearing difficulty. Speech delay also makes this an at-risk child, in whom tubes may be offered when effusion is unlikely to resolve quickly. Per the 2016 OME guideline, do not treat effusion with steroids, antibiotics, antihistamines or decongestants.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-aom-ome-tympanostomy-decisions': {
        "curveball": 'This child has had four infections in 6 months, but today both ears are clear without effusion. Does the guideline support tubes?',
        "curveball_answer": 'No. The 2022 guideline says clinicians should not place tubes for recurrent acute otitis media when no middle-ear effusion is present in either ear at the time of assessment. Tubes are offered for recurrent infection, defined as 3 episodes in 6 months or 4 in 12 months with one in the last 6 months, only when effusion is present. Re-examine at a later visit.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-ankyloglossia-maxillary-frenulum': {
        "curveball": 'After frenotomy for a breastfeeding infant, the latch does not improve. What should you consider next?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Lack of improvement often means the frenulum was not the main problem. Reassess with a lactation consultant for positioning, milk supply, maternal nipple problems, infant oral-motor or neurologic issues and reflux, and check for reattachment. The 2020 AAO-HNS consensus statement cautions against routine maxillary frenulum release for breastfeeding problems, and so-called posterior ties remain controversial, so avoid repeated releases without clear function findings.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-branchial-cleft-anomalies': {
        "curveball": 'A 45-year-old has a new cystic lateral neck mass presumed to be a branchial cleft cyst. What must you do before excising it?',
        "curveball_answer": 'In an adult, a cystic lateral neck mass is a metastatic node until proven otherwise, classically from HPV-associated oropharyngeal cancer. Per the 2017 neck mass guideline, examine the head and neck fully including laryngoscopy, obtain contrast CT or MRI, and perform fine-needle aspiration with p16 or HPV testing rather than open excision. Do not prescribe antibiotics without signs of infection.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-branchial-cleft-anomalies': {
        "curveball": 'A child has recurrent left-sided neck abscesses with acute suppurative thyroiditis. Which anomaly, where does its tract begin, and what treatment exists?',
        "curveball_answer": 'This is a third or fourth branchial pouch sinus, which begins at the apex of the pyriform sinus and occurs on the left in most cases. Confirm with direct laryngoscopy and imaging after the infection settles. Endoscopic cauterization of the sinus opening is an effective first-line treatment; open excision, often with partial thyroidectomy, is reserved for failures.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-button-battery-ingestion': {
        "curveball": 'While a child who swallowed a button battery awaits endoscopy, what can be given to reduce injury, and what are the limits?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Honey, about 10 mL every 10 minutes up to six doses in children over 1 year, can be given at home, and sucralfate in hospital, if ingestion was within about 12 hours. They coat the battery and slow alkaline injury. Never delay removal for them, and do not give them with suspected perforation or sepsis. Some centers irrigate the injury with dilute acetic acid after removal.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-button-battery-ingestion': {
        "curveball": 'After removal, which delayed complications must you watch for, and how is monitoring usually structured?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Injury progresses for days to weeks after removal. Watch for esophageal perforation, mediastinitis, tracheoesophageal fistula, aortoesophageal fistula announced by a sentinel bleed, vocal fold paralysis and stricture. Significant injury needs admission, imaging such as CT or MRI to judge depth and proximity to the aorta, and a contrast study before feeding. Any hematemesis is a surgical emergency.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-choanal-atresia': {
        "curveball": 'A newborn with bilateral choanal atresia also has coloboma and a heart defect. What syndrome is likely, and how does it change planning?',
        "curveball_answer": 'CHARGE syndrome, usually from CHD7 variants: coloboma, heart defects, choanal atresia, growth and developmental delay, genital and ear anomalies. Planning changes include cardiac evaluation before anesthesia, assessment for other airway problems and aspiration, cranial nerve dysfunction, temporal bone imaging for absent semicircular canals and cochlear nerve deficiency, and counseling about higher restenosis after repair.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-choanal-atresia': {
        "curveball": "How do you secure this newborn's airway while planning repair, and how do you confirm the diagnosis?",
        "curveball_answer": 'Keep the mouth open with an oral airway or a McGovern nipple, a nipple with an enlarged hole taped in place; intubate if that fails. Confirm with nasal endoscopy after suctioning and decongestion, and CT of the nasal cavity, which shows bony, mixed or membranous atresia and a medialized lateral wall. Endoscopic transnasal repair is the usual approach; stenting remains debated.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-cleft-craniofacial-otologic-airway-care': {
        "curveball": 'A child with 22q11.2 deletion needs pharyngeal flap surgery for velopharyngeal insufficiency. What must you check before operating?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Look for medially displaced internal carotid arteries in the posterior pharyngeal wall, common in 22q11.2 deletion; check for pulsations on nasopharyngoscopy and obtain imaging such as MRA. Screen for obstructive sleep apnea, since flaps can worsen it, and review cardiac anomalies, hypocalcemia and immune status with the craniofacial team before anesthesia.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-cleft-craniofacial-otologic-airway-care': {
        "curveball": 'Should this infant receive tubes at the time of palate repair, and how does the 2022 tubes guideline handle cleft palate?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Many cleft teams place tubes during palatoplasty to restore hearing during speech development, since effusion is nearly universal. The 2022 guideline lists cleft palate as an at-risk condition in which tubes may be offered when effusion is unlikely to resolve quickly, but it does not mandate routine placement. Monitor hearing and individualize, weighing otorrhea and perforation risk.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-cleft-lip-palate-ent-surgical-fundamentals': {
        "curveball": 'What if this infant had Pierre Robin sequence with airway obstruction? How does that alter palatoplasty timing and planning?',
        "curveball_answer": 'In Robin sequence, micrognathia and glossoptosis obstruct the airway, and palate closure narrows it further. Delay palatoplasty until the airway is stable, sometimes after mandibular distraction or tongue-lip adhesion; polysomnography helps decide. Plan close postoperative airway monitoring, possibly with a nasal trumpet, and evaluate for Stickler syndrome with an eye exam, given the risk of myopia and retinal detachment.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-congenital-cmv-hearing-loss': {
        "curveball": "This infant's only finding is sensorineural hearing loss. Should valganciclovir be offered, and what follow-up is needed?",
        "curveball_answer": 'Six months of valganciclovir started within the first month improves hearing and developmental outcomes in symptomatic congenital CMV. For isolated hearing loss, treatment is increasingly offered, though the evidence is weaker, so use shared decision-making with infectious disease. Monitor blood counts for neutropenia. Because CMV loss can be delayed or progressive, continue serial audiology through early childhood.',
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-congenital-hearing-loss-genetics': {
        "curveball": 'Which genetic test is first-line for nonsyndromic congenital sensorineural loss, and what result or history would prompt an ECG?',
        "curveball_answer": 'A comprehensive hearing loss gene panel, which includes GJB2 and GJB6, is first-line for nonsyndromic cases, along with CMV testing when timing allows. An ECG is recommended for congenital severe-to-profound loss of unknown cause, or with syncope or family sudden death, to detect Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome, a recessive long QT syndrome from KCNQ1 or KCNE1 variants, treated with beta-blockers.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-congenital-hearing-loss-genetics': {
        "curveball": 'A child with congenital hearing loss develops night blindness in adolescence. Which syndrome is likely, and how does it change rehabilitation planning?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Usher syndrome: sensorineural loss with retinitis pigmentosa causing night blindness and progressive field loss. Type 1 has congenital profound deafness and absent vestibular function, type 2 moderate-to-severe loss, and type 3 progressive loss. Because vision loss will erode lipreading and sign access, early cochlear implantation and strong auditory rehabilitation are prioritized, with ophthalmology follow-up.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-congenital-neck-masses': {
        "curveball": 'Before excising this thyroglossal duct cyst, what imaging do you obtain, and which operation lowers recurrence?',
        "curveball_answer": "Ultrasound confirms a normally located thyroid, so you do not remove ectopic thyroid that may be the patient's only functioning tissue. The Sistrunk procedure, removing the cyst, the central hyoid and the tract toward the foramen cecum, greatly lowers recurrence compared with simple excision. Send the specimen to pathology because papillary carcinoma occasionally arises in the cyst.",
    },
    'cc-v112-rec-pediatric-otolaryngology-croup-vs-epiglottitis': {
        "curveball": 'A vaccinated child with croup-like illness has high fever, looks toxic, and does not respond to nebulized epinephrine. What should you suspect?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Bacterial tracheitis, often from Staphylococcus aureus, typically follows a viral croup-like illness, with high fever, toxicity and poor response to racemic epinephrine. Secure the airway in a controlled setting, perform rigid bronchoscopy to remove purulent membranes and obtain cultures, and start IV antibiotics covering S. aureus including MRSA. Many children need intubation.',
    },
    'cc-v112-mgt-pediatric-otolaryngology-croup-vs-epiglottitis': {
        "curveball": 'After nebulized epinephrine and dexamethasone, she is calm without stridor at rest. When can she go home, and what return precautions apply?',
        "curveball_answer": 'Observe for a few hours after epinephrine, commonly 2 to 4, because its effect wears off and stridor can rebound. Discharge if there is no stridor at rest, normal oxygen saturation, good oral intake and reliable caregivers. Return for stridor at rest, retractions, drooling, cyanosis or lethargy. Recurrent or atypical croup warrants airway endoscopy for subglottic stenosis or hemangioma.',
    },
}
